ALL AND ONLY THESE INITIAL OPERATIONS ARE PERMITTED.

Status: closed INITIAL SCALAR SUBSET of ARMv7-A T32, authored in English.
It is NOT a complete catalogue of all T32 encodings or extensions.
Source of architectural encoding and exceptional behavior: ARM DDI 0406C.d.
Read "Given a machine - objects and execution.english" first.
Read "Instruction set architecture - sources and unfulfilled coverage.english"
for the authoritative reference and list of unfulfilled verification obligations.

CONTRACT OF PERMISSION.

An English Major C program whose architecture grant names THIS document is
allowed to request exactly the operation families numbered below, and no
others. A family is granted only in the legal ARMv7-A T32 instruction forms
documented in ARM DDI 0406C.d, subject to the additional restrictions below.
The ARM mnemonics in parentheses are mappings for later verification, not the
names of the English Major C operations. Aliases are not automatically granted
as independent operations. There is currently NO validated assembler,
elaborator, encoding checker or compiler. An unsupported or unverified
operand/encoding combination must be REJECTED, never guessed.

A "general register" in this first grant denotes only one of R0 through R12.
Use of SP, LR, or PC by an operation is disallowed except where specifically
stated. A source operand may be a general register or an immediate literal
ONLY when that numbered operation and the cited instruction encoding permit
that kind of operand. Arbitrary constants cannot be assumed to fit one
instruction. Source and destination aliasing is allowed ONLY as permitted by
the relevant exact architectural instruction form.

A register holds a bit pattern of length 32. "Thirty-two-bit addition" below
means the low 32 bits of the full mathematical sum unless an operation
explicitly retains more. Negative integers use two's-complement
interpretation. The four elementary condition flags are N (result bit 31),
Z (result equals zero), C (carry out of addition or no-borrow from subtraction,
as applicable), and V (signed overflow). An operation described as
"without changing condition flags" MUST preserve N, Z, C and V. A later
lowering may not choose an instruction form that sets flags by default
unless the source operation authorizes that effect.

A memory address is a virtual byte address, not a part number or physical
DRAM location. All memory effects include the ARM-defined address
calculation, size, alignment, access checks, memory type, endianness,
writeback restrictions and fault behavior for the selected encoding.
Neither a failed access nor an architecture-invalid encoding is a no-op.
An IT block condition applies only where legal for the mapped encoding.

ARITHMETIC, COMPARISON, AND BIT OPERATIONS.

Operation 01. "Copy all thirty-two bits from one general register into
another general register without changing condition flags."
Mapping: MOV (register), with a legal non-flag-setting T32 form.
Effect: destination becomes source; all other general registers and
N, Z, C, V remain unchanged.

Operation 02. "Replace one general register with a value represented by
one legal encoded immediate operand without changing condition flags."
Mapping: MOV (immediate), exact form selected by encoder.
Effect: destination becomes the zero-extended, rotated, or otherwise
architecturally constructed immediate value defined by THAT encoding.
This is NOT an arbitrary signed 32-bit literal facility.

Operation 03. "Write an explicitly encoded sixteen-bit constant as the
low sixteen bits of a general register, making its high sixteen bits zero."
Mapping: MOVW. Effect: destination becomes unsigned immediate[15:0].

Operation 04. "Replace only the high sixteen bits of one general register
with an explicitly encoded sixteen-bit constant."
Mapping: MOVT. Effect: destination[31:16] is the immediate; destination
[15:0] is preserved. No flags change.

Operation 05. "Add the thirty-two-bit contents of two general registers
and retain the low thirty-two bits without changing condition flags."
Mapping: non-flag-setting ADD (register). The encoding must actually
permit flag preservation; do not silently substitute a narrow ADDS form.

Operation 06. "Add the contents of a general register to a legally
encodable immediate integer and retain the low thirty-two bits without
changing condition flags."
Mapping: non-flag-setting ADD (immediate). The permitted literal range
and encoding determine availability.

Operation 07. "Add two thirty-two-bit operands, retain the low
thirty-two bits, and update negative, zero, carry, and overflow flags."
Mapping: ADDS (legal register or immediate T32 form). N and Z describe
the stored result; C is the unsigned carry out; V is signed overflow.

Operation 08. "Add the two thirty-two-bit operands together with the
incoming carry flag, retain the low thirty-two bits, and preserve flags."
Mapping: non-flag-setting ADC (register or legal immediate form).
Result = (first + second + old C) modulo 2^32.

Operation 09. "Add the two thirty-two-bit operands together with incoming
carry, retain the low thirty-two bits, and update arithmetic flags."
Mapping: ADCS. Its old C is read BEFORE computing the new flags.

Operation 10. "Subtract the second thirty-two-bit operand from the first,
retain the low thirty-two bits, and preserve the condition flags."
Mapping: non-flag-setting SUB, in a legal T32 form.

Operation 11. "Subtract the second operand from the first, retain the low
thirty-two bits, and update negative, zero, no-borrow, and overflow flags."
Mapping: SUBS. A result with no unsigned borrow sets C; V records
signed overflow.

Operation 12. "Subtract the second operand and any incoming borrow from
the first, retaining the low thirty-two bits without changing flags."
Mapping: non-flag-setting SBC. Result = first - second - (1 - old C),
modulo 2^32.

Operation 13. "Subtract the first operand from the second and retain the
low thirty-two bits without changing flags."
Mapping: non-flag-setting RSB in a legal form. This reverses SUB's
operand order and is not a semantic alias for SUB with unchanged operands.

Operation 14. "Compare two encoded operands by performing subtraction
for condition flags without retaining the arithmetic result."
Mapping: CMP. Set N/Z/C/V as for first - second; preserve source
operands. Equality is then Z=1. Signed less-than is N!=V; unsigned
less-than is C=0.

Operation 15. "Compare two encoded operands by performing addition
for condition flags without retaining the arithmetic result."
Mapping: CMN. Set N/Z/C/V as for first + second.

Operation 16. "Test the bitwise intersection of two operands, preserving
their original values and setting comparison flags as specified."
Mapping: TST. The Z flag describes whether the bitwise AND is zero.
The exact instruction's shifter-carry rule applies; V is preserved.
Do NOT infer C from arbitrary arithmetic.

Operation 17. "Produce the bitwise logical intersection of the two
specified thirty-two-bit values without changing condition flags."
Mapping: non-flag-setting AND. Destination gets first AND second.

Operation 18. "Produce the bitwise inclusive union of two specified
thirty-two-bit values without changing condition flags."
Mapping: non-flag-setting ORR. Destination gets first OR second.

Operation 19. "Produce the bitwise exclusive-or of two specified
thirty-two-bit values without changing condition flags."
Mapping: non-flag-setting EOR. Destination gets first XOR second.

Operation 20. "Clear in the first value every bit selected by a set bit
in the second value, without changing condition flags."
Mapping: non-flag-setting BIC. Destination gets first AND (NOT second).

Operation 21. "Invert each of the thirty-two bits of the selected source
value and store the result without changing condition flags."
Mapping: non-flag-setting MVN. Destination gets bitwise NOT source.

Operation 22. "Shift a thirty-two-bit value left by the encoded number of
bit positions and insert zero bits at the right."
Mapping: LSL, non-flag-setting form. The result is truncated to 32 bits.
Immediate/register count forms and special count cases are constrained
by the ARM reference; they are NOT assumed interchangeable.

Operation 23. "Shift an unsigned thirty-two-bit value right by the
encoded number of bit positions and insert zero bits on the left."
Mapping: LSR, non-flag-setting form, with ARM-defined count semantics.

Operation 24. "Shift a signed thirty-two-bit value right by the encoded
number of bit positions, replicating its original sign bit."
Mapping: ASR, non-flag-setting form, with ARM-defined count semantics.

Operation 25. "Rotate the thirty-two-bit value right by the encoded
number of bit positions without losing rotated bits."
Mapping: ROR, non-flag-setting form. RRX and special carry-based cases
are NOT automatically included in this name.

Operation 26. "Count the consecutive zero bits starting with the most
significant bit of a general register."
Mapping: CLZ. Destination is an integer between 0 and 32. Input zero
produces 32.

Operation 27. "Multiply the two selected thirty-two-bit values and
retain only the low thirty-two bits without changing condition flags."
Mapping: MUL (non-flag-setting T32 form). This is not a wide product.

Operation 28. "Sign-extend the lowest eight bits of the specified
general register into a thirty-two-bit signed bit pattern."
Mapping: SXTB. No flags change, subject to encoding constraints.
The version with optional rotation, if supported, must be selected
explicitly by the exact instruction form.

Operation 29. "Sign-extend the lowest sixteen bits of the specified
general register into a thirty-two-bit signed bit pattern."
Mapping: SXTH. No flags change, subject to encoding constraints.

Operation 30. "Zero-extend the lowest eight bits of the specified
general register into a thirty-two-bit bit pattern."
Mapping: UXTB. No flags change, subject to encoding constraints.

Operation 31. "Zero-extend the lowest sixteen bits of the specified
general register into a thirty-two-bit bit pattern."
Mapping: UXTH. No flags change, subject to encoding constraints.

MEMORY AND ADDRESS OPERATIONS.

Operation 32. "Load exactly four bytes from one permitted effective
memory address into a general register and interpret them as one
thirty-two-bit word."
Mapping: LDR (immediate/register offset, legal T32 forms). The address
is computed from a permitted base register, offset and addressing mode.
This operation does not grant arbitrary address writeback, privileged
unprivileged-access variants, or a load into PC.

Operation 33. "Load one byte from a permitted effective memory address
and extend it with zero bits into a general register."
Mapping: LDRB. Flags are preserved.

Operation 34. "Load one byte from a permitted effective memory address,
interpret bit seven as the sign, and sign-extend it to thirty-two bits."
Mapping: LDRSB. Flags are preserved.

Operation 35. "Load two consecutive bytes from a permitted effective
memory address as an unsigned halfword and extend it with zeros."
Mapping: LDRH. Flags are preserved.

Operation 36. "Load two consecutive bytes from a permitted effective
memory address as a signed halfword and sign-extend it."
Mapping: LDRSH. Flags are preserved.

Operation 37. "Store exactly four bytes representing the low
thirty-two bits of one general register to a permitted memory address."
Mapping: STR. The exact byte ordering is that of the active machine
and mapping; flags are preserved.

Operation 38. "Store only the least significant eight bits of a general
register to a permitted memory address."
Mapping: STRB. Flags are preserved.

Operation 39. "Store only the least significant sixteen bits of a general
register to a permitted memory address."
Mapping: STRH. Flags are preserved.

Operation 40. "Compute a permitted immediate address relative to
the architectural program-counter operand without loading memory."
Mapping: the TWO-BYTE T32 ADR T1, whose encoding begins with
binary 10100 followed by three bits selecting R0 through R7,
and eight unsigned immediate bits. Calculate the PC operand
as the CURRENT instruction byte address A plus FOUR, then
align that value DOWN to a multiple of FOUR bytes before
adding four times the encoded unsigned eight-bit immediate.
Write the resulting 32-bit value into the selected low
general-purpose register. The permitted displacement is
0 through 1020 bytes inclusive in multiples of four.
Do not read or write data memory at the result address.
Do not branch, overwrite the execution index with the
computed address, modify SP/LR or change N/Z/C/V.
On successful normal execution this instruction occupies
TWO bytes, advancing the execution index from A to A+2.
Its data-register update is conditional upon any LEGAL
enclosing ITSTATE predicate; the instruction's length
and its own encoding do not change when skipped.

For example, when A=0x00001002, PC operand is 0x1006,
but the aligned PC base is 0x1004. Halfword 0xA001
computes R0=0x1008, without a data read or branch.
The complete assigned T1 interval A000..A7FF (2048
low-register and immediate combinations) is recorded in
"Every halfword that forms an address from the execution
position or stack pointer.english" and the associated TSV
in _/fixtures/armv7-a-t32-address-generation-halfwords.tsv.
This name does NOT automatically grant wider or negative-
displacement ADR encodings, A32 PC arithmetic, or
instructions with PC as a destination.

Operation 41. "Load a thirty-two-bit word from an architecture-defined
literal address based upon the current instruction's PC-relative value."
Mapping: the TWO-BYTE T32 LDR (literal) T1 encoding.
The first five bits are 01001; the next three bits
select destination R0 through R7; the last eight bits
are an unsigned literal imm8. Let the current instruction
address be A. Calculate the data address as
Align(A+4,4) + 4*imm8, under the declared 32-bit
address, endian, mapping and access-permission rules.
Read exactly one FOUR-BYTE DATA WORD from that virtual
address and write the assembled 32-bit data value
to the selected register. The register receives
the MEMORY CONTENTS, not the numerical address itself.
The encoded displacement is 0..1020 in increments of four.
This instruction has NO implicit branch, PC write,
SP/LR write or N/Z/C/V update. It has an actual
data-memory READ which may fault; it does not
perform an instruction fetch from the resulting
word address merely by loading its data.
The two-byte sequential successor is A+2 on
normal completion. If a legal IT condition is false,
the ordinary data read and destination write are
suppressed, while instruction sequencing still
accounts for the two-byte encoding and ITSTATE.

For example, at A=0x1002, halfword 0x4901 reads
one data word from Align(0x1006,4)+4 = 0x1008
into R1, subject to the mapping's readable status.
The complete assigned 4800..4FFF region is in
"Every PC-relative data-word load halfword.english"
and _/fixtures/armv7-a-t32-pc-relative-word-load-halfwords.tsv.
LDR literal's other possible ARM encodings or
PC destination variants are NOT licensed by
this single explicitly granted T1 form.

BRANCHING AND EXPLICIT CONTROL FLOW.

Operation 42. "Replace the execution index with the named legal T32
instruction address without modifying the link register."
Mapping: B (unconditional immediate, T32), subject to architecture-defined
conditional-execution state. A specific admitted form is the
TWO-BYTE B T2 encoding whose five highest bits are 11100 and
whose eleven low bits are an encoded immediate imm11.
Interpret imm11 as an ELEVEN-bit two's-complement integer, multiply
it by TWO to get a signed BYTE displacement ranging from -2048
through +2046 inclusive, and add that displacement to the
current instruction's architectural PC-operand base, which for
this form is the instruction address plus FOUR. The resulting
halfword-aligned address must be a verified executable T32
instruction entry. This instruction does not write LR, SP,
R0 through R12, data memory, N, Z, C or V.

OUTSIDE an IT block, an admitted B T2 normally takes
its target without consulting N/Z/C/V.
IN THE LAST SLOT of a legal IT block, the branch's
control-transfer effect occurs ONLY IF the enclosing ITSTATE
condition holds; otherwise ordinary sequential execution
advances the execution index by TWO bytes and consumes
the final IT slot. Putting a B T2 into any earlier IT
slot is architecturally UNPREDICTABLE and REJECTED.
This distinction comes from DDI 0406C.d A8.8.18,
encoding T2; it does NOT apply identically to the
separate T1 conditional B (operation 43).

The complete 2048-halfword encoding region E000..E7FF
is described by "Every short unconditional branch
halfword.english" and the associated row-by-row data
file _/fixtures/armv7-a-t32-unconditional-branch-halfwords.tsv.
For example, E000 has displacement zero and selects
current instruction address plus FOUR if its normal
branch effect occurs; E7FE has displacement -4,
and E7FF has displacement -2, selecting an address
equal to the sequential two-byte successor while
still being a taken branch outside IT.

An ARM disassembler can render E000 as "B" outside IT
but "BEQ" when it is the last slot after IT EQ.
Those are the SAME encoded instruction bytes,
with the effective condition provided by ITSTATE,
not by changing the encoded halfword to a T1
condition-coded B. The exact original bytes,
ITSTATE and program address must be retained.
This admission does NOT automatically grant the
FOUR-BYTE T32 B T4 encoding or any A32 instruction.
An out-of-range address, invalid target state,
non-executable mapping, or unproved entry boundary
must block source-level executable lowering.

Operation 43. "Replace the execution index with the named legal T32
instruction address only if the selected architectural condition holds;
otherwise continue at the sequential successor."
Mapping: B with a condition code. Conditions include equal (Z=1),
not equal (Z=0), unsigned comparisons using C and Z, signed comparisons
using N, V and Z, and the remaining legal ARM condition codes.
The condition code and legal branch encoding are part of this operation,
not an unverified source-level Boolean expression.

A VERIFIED SUBSET of this operation is the 16-bit Thumb B T1 encoding
whose first four bits are binary 1101 and whose next four bits encode
exactly one of the fourteen condition codes 0000 through 1101.
The final eight bits are a signed two's-complement displacement,
scaled by two bytes, giving an even offset from -256 through +254.
The branch target on a true condition is the instruction's byte
address plus FOUR plus that signed offset. A false condition advances
the execution index by TWO bytes. In either case R0..R12, SP, LR
and N/Z/C/V remain unchanged by the branch itself.
A T1 conditional B is architecturally NOT ALLOWED inside an IT
block; entering it in an IT block is UNPREDICTABLE. Permission
for a differently encoded B cannot be inferred from this T1 rule.

Hexadecimal DE00..DEFF belong to the separate UDF T1 class,
not an encoded "always" branch. Hexadecimal DF00..DFFF belong
to SVC T1, not an encoded "never" branch. Neither is granted
as an executable English Major C operation by this entry.
All 4096 encoded halfwords D000..DFFF are enumerated in
_/fixtures/armv7-a-t32-conditional-branch-and-exception-halfwords.tsv
and defined under "Every conditional-branch and supervisor-call
halfword.english". Fourteen flag conditions are separately
cross-checked over all sixteen N/Z/C/V states in
_/fixtures/armv7-a-t32-conditional-branch-flag-truth-table.tsv.

Other ARMv7-A T32 B forms, including wider T32 conditional
branch encodings, still require form-specific validation of their
widths, offset fields, IT restrictions, processor features
and legal destination conditions; this name alone is not
a blanket permission for any mnemonic displayed as B.

Operation 44. "Branch forward to the permitted target if the entire
tested low general-purpose register contains zero, otherwise continue
sequentially."
Mapping: CBZ, the 16-bit T32 form. Its direct register test does NOT
consume N/Z/C/V and does not set them. Only R0 through R7 are admitted
as the tested register. The unsigned displacement is a multiple of two
from zero through 126 bytes relative to the instruction's PC operand
value (the instruction address plus four). Its destination must stay
in T32 state. CBZ cannot be inside an IT block. This is not an
arbitrary signed-value comparison.

Operation 45. "Branch forward to the permitted target if the entire
tested low general-purpose register is nonzero, otherwise continue
sequentially."
Mapping: CBNZ, the 16-bit T32 form. Its permissible tested registers
are R0 through R7; it is forbidden in an IT block. The unsigned
displacement is zero through 126 bytes in even steps from the
instruction's PC operand value (the instruction address plus four).
It neither reads the current condition flags as its predicate nor
changes N/Z/C/V. The same T32 target-state requirement applies
as for CBZ.

Operation 46. "Mark one through four immediately following T32
instructions as conditionally executed according to one condition and,
where legally specified, its inverse."
Mapping: IT (If-Then), T32 T1 encoding with first eight bits
binary 10111111, followed by a four-bit firstcond and a
NONZERO four-bit mask. If firstcond is hexadecimal F,
REJECT as architecturally UNPREDICTABLE. If firstcond
is hexadecimal E (AL), only a mask with exactly ONE
set bit is accepted; all other masks are REJECTED as
UNPREDICTABLE. For firstcond 0 through D, the fifteen
nonzero masks are admitted at the encoding-classification
stage. Together, these are 214 conditionally admissible
encoding patterns, not 240 unconditionally legal programs.
If an IT block is already active, another IT is UNPREDICTABLE
and SHALL be REJECTED. The number of following instruction
SLOTS is four minus the number of trailing zero bits in the
four-bit mask; each slot holds one whole T32 instruction,
which itself may occupy TWO or FOUR bytes. IT changes
the architectural ITSTATE, not the N/Z/C/V flags or an
ordinary general-purpose register, and is itself TWO bytes.
Its exact Then/Else condition sequence is determined by
the architecture's firstcond/mask state machine; decoding
the slot count alone is insufficient.

Every following instruction must separately be permitted
in its actual IT context, including restrictions on
flag-setting forms and PC-changing instructions. In
particular, the TWO-BYTE T1 conditional B encoding
from D000..DDFF is NOT permitted inside IT, even if
it is in the last slot. The exact ITSTATE advancement,
exception entry/return, prohibited branches, and
architectural restrictions SHALL come from ARM DDI
0406C.d. No IT opcode authorizes executing an otherwise
unpermitted native instruction or an unverified instruction
boundary. A following instruction whose legal IT
condition fails has the architecture-defined nonexecuting
effect, while its full instruction encoding still
occupies its actual two- or four-byte instruction span.

The complete 256-halfword IT/hint region BF00..BFFF is
documented under "Every If Then and hint halfword.english"
and individually tabulated in
_/fixtures/armv7-a-t32-if-then-and-hint-halfwords.tsv.
Twenty-six firstcond/mask combinations are REJECTED
as unpredictable despite an independent disassembler's
ability to print mnemonic-like text for those encodings.

Operation 47. "Branch to a permitted immediate T32 function entry and
write the architecturally encoded return address into the link register."
Mapping: BL (immediate). The return address retains the required
Thumb-state indication in LR. This is NOT permission to enter A32
state or an unspecified calling convention.

Operation 48. "Replace the execution index with the instruction
address held in one permitted general-purpose register or in the link
register, after interpreting the least significant bit of that stored
value as the instruction-state indication rather than an address bit."
Mapping: BX (register). R0 through R12 and LR (R14) are permitted
sources only when the selected Thumb encoding permits them; SP (R13)
and PC (R15) are excluded by this English Major C grant. The
selected register's bit zero MUST be one for an admitted T32 target;
the actual byte address is the register value with bit zero cleared.
If bit zero is zero, the physical instruction requests A32 state,
which is disallowed by this profile. BX does not save a new return
address in LR. Consequently BX LR is the explicitly named T32
return mechanism, provided LR holds a valid tagged return address.
No condition flags are changed. A call and return do not imply
a particular ABI or stack discipline.

Operation 49. "Branch to a permitted T32 target obtained from a
register while saving the return location in the link register."
Mapping: BLX (register) limited to target values that remain in
T32 state. All state exchange and link effects must be validated.
BLX forms that enter A32 state are excluded here.

Operation 50. "Read an unsigned entry from an indexed byte table,
double its numerical value to obtain a halfword-scaled forward
displacement, and replace the execution index with the resulting
instruction address relative to the architectural PC operand."
Mapping: TBB (32-bit T32). The base may be R0 through R12 or PC,
but not SP; the index may only be R0 through R12. A PC base means
the instruction's starting address plus four. Read one byte at
[base value + index value]. If that unsigned byte is K, the
branch target is [starting instruction address + four + 2*K].
The entry is data rather than an instruction, and the operation
is prohibited inside an IT block. It cannot change to A32 state.
The exact legality of destination, memory access and operands
must be verified; no automatic bounds check is provided.

Operation 51. "Read an unsigned entry from an indexed halfword
table, double its numerical value to obtain a halfword-scaled
forward displacement, and replace the execution index with the
resulting instruction address relative to the architectural PC."
Mapping: TBH (32-bit T32). The base may be R0 through R12 or PC,
but not SP; the index may only be R0 through R12. A PC base means
the instruction's starting address plus four. Read one
sixteen-bit unsigned halfword at [base value + 2*index value].
If that halfword is K, the branch target is
[starting instruction address + four + 2*K]. This differs
from TBB in both entry width and table-address calculation,
not the factor of two used for its branch target. The
entry is data rather than executable instruction bytes.
TBH is forbidden in an IT block and does not switch to
A32 state. Any memory fault or illegal target must not
be treated as a successful branch.

Operation 52. "Perform an architectural no-operation instruction,
normally advancing the execution index according to its encoding."
Mapping: the specifically permitted TWO-BYTE Thumb T1 NOP
encoding hexadecimal BF00. On ordinary successful execution
the execution index advances by two bytes and this operation
does not modify R0..R12, SP, LR or N/Z/C/V.
A no-op is not a guarantee of zero time, zero power,
or no microarchitectural activity. This operation name
does NOT authorize the separately allocated hints
BF10 (YIELD), BF20 (WFE), BF30 (WFI), BF40 (SEV),
or any of the eleven other BFx0 encodings that
ARM identifies as UNALLOCATED HINTS. Those eleven
can execute as NOPs at the architecture level,
but the ARM specification states software MUST NOT
use them; this closed source profile REJECTS them.
No 32-bit Thumb NOP encoding is granted solely by
sharing an assembler mnemonic with BF00.

ADDITIONAL IMMEDIATE-TO-REGISTER FORM WITH FLAG EFFECTS.

Operation 53. "Write the unsigned eight-bit immediate value explicitly
encoded in this instruction into the selected low general-purpose
register after extending the value with zeros to thirty-two bits;
set the negative and zero condition flags from that result while
preserving the old carry and overflow condition flags."
Mapping: MOVS (immediate), the 16-bit T32 T1 encoding outside an IT
block. The first five bits of the instruction halfword are binary
00100. The next three bits choose a destination register R0
through R7. The lowest eight bits contain a literal between
zero and 255, inclusive. The destination becomes that literal
as a 32-bit value. Negative flag N becomes zero because
its bit 31 is zero. Zero flag Z becomes one exactly when the
literal is zero; otherwise Z becomes zero. Carry flag C and
overflow flag V retain their old values. The 16-bit form
has length TWO bytes. The destination is not SP, LR, or PC.
Only the explicitly flag-setting immediate form OUTSIDE IT
is admitted. A non-flag-setting MOV and a flag-setting
MOVS are separate English operations: 02 cannot be
silently substituted for 53. The behavior and encoding
are subject to DDI 0406C.d; no assembler/compiler
execution has yet been qualified.


STACK AND INCREMENTING MULTIPLE-WORD OPERATIONS.

The following additions distinguish stack pointer arithmetic from actual
memory transfers. A "register set" is an encoded set whose members have
unique register numbers; the textual order in which its names are written
does not change the transfer order. The lowest numbered included register
is associated with the lowest address in the transfer region. A load/store
multiple or stack memory operation can fault partway through a transfer.
No operation in this section promises transactional all-or-nothing
memory effects after an exception. Successful-completion descriptions
apply only when all required accesses complete normally.

Operation 54. "Subtract four bytes for every selected register from
the current thirty-two-bit stack pointer, write each selected register's
original four-byte value into its designated ascending memory location
within that newly reserved stack interval, and retain the lowest
reserved address in the stack pointer."
Mapping: PUSH, 16-bit T32 T1, encoding 1011010 M register_list.
The register_list contains only registers R0 through R7. The M bit
adds LR (R14) when M is one; neither PC nor SP may be stored by
this operation. At least one register including optional LR must
be selected. Let count be the number of selected registers and
let oldSP be the starting stack pointer. For successful execution,
newSP = oldSP - 4*count (modulo 2^32). Store the register with the
lowest register number at newSP, the next selected number at
newSP+4, and so on, with LR last if selected. Preserve the
original source register values and N/Z/C/V. No control transfer
occurs; the execution index advances by two bytes. Require a
word-aligned, writable region and applicable OS stack permission
facts. A failure is an exception, not a successful partial PUSH.
This initial profile permits this 16-bit encoding ONLY; larger
T32 PUSH forms are not implied by this name.

Operation 55. "Load one word for each selected low general-purpose
register from consecutive stack locations, starting at the
original stack pointer and ascending by four bytes between
selected registers; then advance the stack pointer by four
bytes per selected register."
Mapping: POP, 16-bit T32 T1, encoding 1011110 0 register_list.
The list is a nonempty subset of R0 through R7. The lowest
numbered selected destination receives the word at oldSP,
and each successively higher numbered destination receives
the next word in ascending memory order. On normal completion
SP = oldSP + 4*count (modulo 2^32). Preserve N/Z/C/V
and LR. No control transfer occurs. No PC or LR list bit
is present in this permitted form. Memory must be readable
and word aligned. This is not a generic 32-bit POP form.

Operation 56. "Load one word for each selected low general-purpose
register from consecutive stack locations, then load an
additional final word as a tagged instruction-state target,
advance the stack pointer past all loaded words, and branch
only if that target requests a permitted T32 instruction
address."
Mapping: POP, 16-bit T32 T1 with P=1, encoding
1011110 1 register_list. R0 through R7 may be included;
the PC entry is always included, even if no low register
is selected. Let count be the number of selected low
registers plus one for PC. The low registers load in
numeric order from oldSP upward. The last word comes
from oldSP+4*(count-1) and is used for LoadWritePC.
The target word must have bit zero equal to ONE for T32
state, and its address after removing that state bit must
be an approved executable instruction boundary.
A target word whose bit zero is ZERO requests A32,
which this language profile rejects; an encoding whose
lowest two bits equal binary 10 is additionally invalid
for the architectural interworking load.
The successful stack pointer is oldSP+4*count.
This is a control transfer, not mere sequential flow.
It does not directly overwrite LR, does not change
N/Z/C/V, and does not automatically ensure that
the restored calling convention was valid.
The PC-changing operation must be outside an IT block
or satisfy the architecture's last-slot restriction.

Operation 57. "Store the original thirty-two-bit contents
of each selected low general-purpose register to a
consecutive aligned memory word, starting at the address
originally held in a separate low general-purpose base
register and ascending by four bytes for each register,
then advance that base register by the total stored size."
Mapping: STM or STMIA, 16-bit T32 T1, encoding
11000 Rn register_list. The base Rn is R0 through R7.
The register_list is a nonempty subset of R0 through R7.
For this RESTRICTED English operation, the base register
MUST NOT be included in the transferred set, even though
some native encodings can legally store a base register
in specific circumstances. Let N be the count of
selected registers; for normal completion, the word
at oldBase+4*k contains the value of the kth
selected register in ascending register-number order,
for k=0 through N-1. The base register becomes
oldBase+4*N modulo 2^32. Flags, SP and LR remain
unchanged. No PC entry or PC-changing effect is granted.
The encoding occupies TWO bytes; the addresses must be
word aligned and writable. Base overlap is refused
instead of guessing the architecture's exceptional
special cases.

Operation 58. "Read consecutive aligned thirty-two-bit
memory words beginning at the address in a separate low
general-purpose base register, put them into the selected
low general-purpose registers in ascending register-number
order, and advance the base register by four bytes for
every transferred word."
Mapping: LDM or LDMIA, 16-bit T32 T1, encoding
11001 Rn register_list. The base Rn is R0 through R7,
and the nonempty register_list contains only R0
through R7. The base register MUST NOT be in the
destination register set for THIS English operation.
This deliberately chooses the form whose base
writeback is defined, rather than the T1 case in
which the base is among destinations and writeback
does not occur. On normal completion each
destination gets its assigned word and the base
becomes oldBase+4*count modulo 2^32. Flags, SP
and LR remain unchanged. No branch occurs and
the instruction occupies TWO bytes. Memory must
be readable, mapped, and word aligned.

Operation 59. "Advance the architectural stack pointer
by four times the explicitly encoded unsigned
seven-bit integer without changing condition flags
or reading or writing any data memory."
Mapping: 16-bit T32 ADD SP, SP, #immediate (T2 form),
encoding 101100000 imm7, hexadecimal base 0xB000.
The immediate is 4*imm7, from zero through 508 inclusive.
For normal execution SP becomes oldSP+immediate
modulo 2^32. N, Z, C, V are unchanged. This is
not a memory-access operation and cannot assert
that the new pointer points to mapped writable
stack memory. This operation occupies two bytes.
Any ABI-imposed alignment obligation is separate.

Operation 60. "Decrease the architectural stack pointer
by four times the explicitly encoded unsigned
seven-bit integer without changing condition flags
or reading or writing any data memory."
Mapping: 16-bit T32 SUB SP, SP, #immediate,
encoding 101100001 imm7, hexadecimal base 0xB080.
The immediate is 4*imm7, from zero through 508
inclusive. For normal execution SP becomes
oldSP-immediate modulo 2^32, N/Z/C/V are preserved,
and the instruction occupies two bytes. The
decrement alone does NOT store any register, initialize
the reserved stack interval, or prove that the
new SP is a valid address. A subsequent load or
store must separately satisfy memory permissions.


WIDE THUMB MULTIPLE-WORD OPERATIONS, WITHOUT PC TRANSFERS.

The next four operations are not simply spelling changes for operations
57 and 58. They permit T32 FOUR-BYTE encodings and explicitly distinguish
the two independent effects of a multiple-word memory transfer and base
register writeback. Their first halfword announces a 32-bit Thumb
instruction; the second halfword is part of the SAME instruction, not the
next instruction in the stream.

In operations 61-64, the base is a general register R0 through R12.
The nonempty register set may contain R0 through R12 and LR (R14)
but MUST NOT contain SP or PC and MUST contain at least TWO DISTINCT
members. Even when the selected native encoding could support a base
overlap in special circumstances, THIS profile forbids the base
register from belonging to the selected register set. The encoded
register mask, not the order in which the user writes the members,
controls the association of register number with ascending address.
The selected operation must have legal T32 operands and mode, and the
exact encoding is still subject to the pinned ARM DDI 0406C.d.
The user-visible flags N, Z, C and V are preserved. All addresses
must meet word alignment and access requirements; multiword memory
accesses are not assumed atomic or rollback-safe under exceptions.

Operation 61. "Beginning at the memory address in a selected
general-purpose base register, copy the original thirty-two-bit
value of every member of a permitted register set into consecutive
four-byte memory words ordered by ascending register number,
without changing the value of the base register afterward."
Mapping: 32-bit Thumb STMIA (T2 form) with the W writeback bit
equal to ZERO. At a base value B with K selected registers,
store the smallest register's old value at B, the next at
B+4, and the final at B+4*(K-1). After successful transfers,
leave the base unchanged. Each selected source register is
preserved, including LR if it is selected. No PC transfer
and no condition flag update occurs. The instruction occupies
FOUR bytes, so ordinary sequential execution advances the
execution index by FOUR, not TWO. A concrete permitted example
is the halfword pair 0xE884,0x4800, storing {R11,LR} with
base R4 and NO base writeback. This form does not itself
establish any return address.

Operation 62. "Beginning at the memory address in a selected
general-purpose base register, copy the original thirty-two-bit
value of every member of a permitted register set into consecutive
four-byte memory words ordered by ascending register number,
and then increase the base register by four bytes per member."
Mapping: 32-bit Thumb STMIA (T2 form) with W equal to ONE.
Successful memory effects are as for operation 61, but the base
becomes B+4*K modulo 2^32. Flags remain unchanged and the
instruction occupies FOUR bytes. A concrete permitted halfword
pair is 0xE8A4,0x4800, storing the original values of
{R11,LR} through base R4 and increasing R4 by eight bytes.
The initial value of R4 must not appear in the selected set.

Operation 63. "Read consecutive four-byte memory words beginning
four bytes times the number of selected registers below the
starting value of a general-purpose base register, assign the
read values to the selected registers in ascending register-number
order, and leave the base register unchanged after the reads."
Mapping: 32-bit Thumb LDMDB (T1 form) with W equal to ZERO,
the Decrement Before multiple-load addressing mode. Let B be
the old base and K the number of selected registers. The
first word, read at B-4*K, goes into the smallest selected
register. The last word, read at B-4, goes into the highest
selected register. The base is preserved. If LR is selected,
LR receives the word assigned to it, but NO BRANCH happens
merely because LR is loaded. The instruction occupies FOUR
bytes, preserves condition flags and does not write PC.
A concrete permitted halfword pair is 0xE914,0x4800,
meaning base R4, set {R11,LR}, and NO base writeback.

Operation 64. "Read consecutive four-byte memory words beginning
four bytes times the number of selected registers below the
starting value of a general-purpose base register, assign the
read values to the selected registers in ascending register-number
order, and then decrease the base register by four bytes per member."
Mapping: 32-bit Thumb LDMDB (T1 form) with W equal to ONE.
On successful completion, the loads are as in operation 63
and the base becomes B-4*K modulo 2^32. Flag state is
preserved. The instruction occupies FOUR bytes and
does NOT branch merely because it may have loaded LR.
A concrete permitted halfword pair is 0xE934,0x4800,
loading {R11,LR} through R4 and changing R4 by negative
eight bytes. Its original R4 must not be among destinations.


BIT REARRANGEMENT AND EXPLICIT EXTRACTION OF REGISTER FIELDS.

The next eight operation names expose exactly which bits of a register
are used, which bits are overwritten, and whether sign extension occurs.
The values manipulated by these operations are REGISTER DATA. Merely
rearranging an instruction-looking bit pattern does not execute it.
The selected encodings are Thumb state and are specifically restricted
below. None of these operations reads data memory, writes data memory,
alters SP or LR, changes the instruction state, or changes N/Z/C/V.
A normally executed instruction advances the execution index by its
actual two-byte or four-byte length. An instruction inside an
otherwise legal IT block is subject to its existing IT condition.
Architectural encoding legality remains governed by DDI 0406C.d.

Operation 65. "Reverse the positions of the four successive eight-bit
portions of a selected thirty-two-bit general-register value, so that
the original least-significant eight-bit portion of the register
becomes the highest-significance portion of the result, and store the
rearranged thirty-two bits without changing the condition flags."
Mapping: REV, 16-bit T32 T1 form only, with source Rm and destination Rd
each selected from R0 through R7. Its halfword has the form
1011101000 Rm(three bits) Rd(three bits), also expressible as
0xBA00 + (Rm-number << 3) + Rd-number. Bits 7:0 and bits 31:24
exchange places; bits 15:8 and bits 23:16 exchange places.
A source value 0x12345678 produces 0x78563412. This instruction
does not change system endianness or memory byte order.
Length: two bytes. Example: 0xBA0A means REV R2, R1.

Operation 66. "Reverse the order of the two eight-bit portions inside
each of the two sixteen-bit portions of a thirty-two-bit register,
without moving a bit from the lower halfword into the upper halfword
or from the upper halfword into the lower halfword."
Mapping: REV16, 16-bit T32 T1 form only, with Rm and Rd each R0-R7.
Encoding: 0xBA40 + (Rm-number << 3) + Rd-number. For an input
0x12345678, return 0x34127856. This changes only the destination
register, leaves N/Z/C/V unchanged, and has length two bytes.
Example: 0xBA63 means REV16 R3, R4.

Operation 67. "Take only the original least significant sixteen bits
of a thirty-two-bit register, interchange their lower and upper
eight-bit portions, then copy the sign bit of the resulting sixteen-bit
value into all sixteen higher destination bits."
Mapping: REVSH, 16-bit T32 T1 form only, with Rm and Rd each R0-R7.
Encoding: 0xBAC0 + (Rm-number << 3) + Rd-number. If the original
low sixteen bits are hexadecimal 0x80FF, the swapped halfword is
0xFF80, whose highest bit is one. The result is 0xFFFFFF80, the
32-bit two's-complement bit representation of negative 128.
The original upper sixteen source bits do not influence the result.
Flags remain unchanged. Length: two bytes. Example: 0xBAF5
means REVSH R5, R6.

Operation 68. "Reverse the order of all thirty-two individual bits of
one general register, putting original bit zero at destination bit
thirty-one, original bit one at destination bit thirty, and so on."
Mapping: RBIT, 32-bit T32 T1 form; Rd and Rm are restricted to
R0 through R12 and neither SP nor PC is accepted. The mapping
is a single FOUR-byte instruction, not a two-byte REV. A source
0x0000000D, whose lowest four bits read 1101 from high to low,
produces 0xB0000000. Preserve N/Z/C/V. Example halfword pair
0xFA98,0xF7A8 encodes RBIT R7,R8. A data reversal does not
select a new branch target merely because the result can
represent an executable address.

Operation 69. "Replace exactly a specified contiguous nonempty
interval of bits in one thirty-two-bit destination register with
zeros while retaining all bits outside that interval exactly."
Mapping: BFC, 32-bit T32 T1 form only. Rd is one of R0 through
R12. Let first_bit be an integer 0..31. Let number_of_bits be an
integer 1..(32-first_bit). All first_bit through
first_bit+number_of_bits-1 inclusive are cleared; all other
destination bits are unchanged. The encoded field describes
the highest bit position, rather than directly storing the
requested count. R0..R12 are legal destinations, not SP or PC.
There is no source register. Flags preserved. Length four bytes.
A destination 0xA5A5FFFF with first_bit=8 and number_of_bits=8
becomes 0xA5A500FF. Halfwords 0xF36F,0x230F encode
BFC R3,#8,#8.

Operation 70. "Replace the selected contiguous destination bit interval
with the same number of consecutive least significant bits of a
selected source register, preserving destination bits outside
the interval and ignoring source bits above the requested width."
Mapping: BFI, 32-bit T32 T1 form only. Both destination Rd and
source Rn are in R0 through R12; neither SP nor PC is permitted.
Require first_bit in 0..31 and number_of_bits in
1..(32-first_bit). The low number_of_bits source bits are shifted
left by first_bit and inserted only into that interval of the old
destination value. The old source and destination values are
read before overwriting Rd, including when Rn and Rd are the
same physical register. N/Z/C/V are preserved. Length four bytes.
If destination was 0x12345678, source was 0x000000AB,
first_bit=8, count=8, the destination becomes 0x1234AB78.
Halfwords 0xF362,0x230F encode BFI R3,R2,#8,#8.

Operation 71. "Read a declared contiguous nonempty interval of bits
from one thirty-two-bit source register, place its least-significant
selected bit into destination bit zero, place the remaining selected
bits consecutively above it, and set every higher destination bit
to zero."
Mapping: UBFX, 32-bit T32 T1 form only. Source and destination
registers are each R0 through R12. Require first_bit 0..31,
number_of_bits 1..(32-first_bit). The instruction encodes
number_of_bits-1, not the literal width itself. Preserve the
condition flags. If source is 0x000005A0, first_bit=4,
count=7, the result is 0x0000005A. Four-byte encoding
0xF3C5,0x1406 represents UBFX R4,R5,#4,#7.
There is no memory access and no implicit signed interpretation.

Operation 72. "Read a declared contiguous nonempty interval of bits
from one thirty-two-bit source register, shift that bit interval
down to destination bit zero, and fill every destination bit
above the interval with copies of the selected interval's
highest bit."
Mapping: SBFX, 32-bit T32 T1 form only, with source/destination
in R0 through R12, first_bit 0..31, number_of_bits
1..(32-first_bit). The sign source is bit number
first_bit+number_of_bits-1 of the ORIGINAL source,
not original bit 31 unless that bit belongs to the selection.
The encoding contains number_of_bits-1. Flags are preserved.
If source is 0x000000E0, first_bit=2, and number_of_bits=6,
the extracted six-bit value is 111000 binary, representing
negative eight; the destination becomes 0xFFFFFFF8.
Example halfwords 0xF347,0x0685 encode SBFX R6,R7,#2,#6.
Length four bytes; it does not change SP, LR, or execution state.

ADDITIONAL LOW-REGISTER STACK-RELATIVE ADDRESS CONSTRUCTION.

Operation 73. "Take the existing thirty-two-bit numerical value in
the stack-pointer register, add an explicitly encoded unsigned
integer representing between zero and one thousand twenty bytes
in multiples of four, and write that sum into one selected
low general-purpose register without changing the stack pointer,
any comparison flag, or any data memory."
Mapping: TWO-BYTE T32 ADD (SP plus immediate) encoding T1.
The highest five bits are binary 10101. Three destination
bits select only R0 through R7. The final eight bits
represent imm8 as an unsigned number from zero through
255, and the actual BYTE increment is four times imm8.
For current SP value S, destination Rd becomes
(S + 4*imm8) modulo 2^32. SP remains EXACTLY S,
not the computed destination. R0 through R12 other
than Rd, LR, memory and the comparison flags N/Z/C/V
are preserved. Normal execution advances the
execution index by TWO bytes rather than branching
or loading from the calculated address. The resulting
bit pattern alone does not prove the destination
denotes readable, writable or mapped memory.
Any enclosing ITSTATE predicate must be LEGAL
and governs whether the register assignment occurs.

This is NOT operation 59, which increases SP itself;
nor operation 60, which decreases SP. For example,
with SP=0x2000, 0xA801 writes R0=0x2004 but leaves
SP=0x2000. It does not perform a PUSH, POP,
stack allocation or a data-memory access.
All 2048 halfwords A800..AFFF are enumerated
under the existing A000..AFFF address-generation
source and fixture. This permission excludes
wider T32 SP-immediate encodings by default.

BOUNDARIES AND REJECTIONS.

This operation grant contains exactly SEVENTY-THREE numbered entries.
An implementation claiming that it supports this grant SHALL verify
the count, unique names, exact encodings and legal operand variants;
the existence of this file alone is not that verification.

Operations OUTSIDE the exact forms named by this numbered grant
are NOT authorized for direct English Major C source use.
The grant now contains PARTICULAR narrow PUSH, POP, LDMIA, STMIA,
and SP-arithmetic forms, and PARTICULAR wide STMIA and LDMDB forms.
It does not authorize other PUSH/POP aliases, register-list
combinations, addressing variants, or exceptions merely by sharing
an assembler mnemonic. Exclusive/atomic memory operations, DMB/DSB/ISB,
supervisor calls, coprocessor operations, VFP, Advanced SIMD/NEON,
division, saturation, arbitrary native-code execution, privileged
exception handling, mode-changing instructions, and every other
unnamed machine instruction remain outside this profile.

Architectural syntaxes, aliases, widths and encodings cannot be freely
exchanged. Some 16-bit Thumb forms set flags when 32-bit Thumb forms
can preserve them, and some forms may be unavailable in IT blocks.
An exact machine encoding, or a verified construction of several
permitted encodings, must be shown before an English operation is
considered executable. Anything UNDEFINED, UNPREDICTABLE,
implementation-defined but undeclared, absent from target features,
or forbidden by user-mode privileges MUST fail rather than silently
relaxing this contract.

A program wishing to perform an operation not on this list must select
a new explicitly versioned profile, give that operation a full long
English name and a verified architectural mapping, and retain the
reason it was admitted. The hardware architecture reference is
not automatically a blanket grant.

SOURCE-LEVEL SELECTION IS NOT COMPILER ACCEPTANCE.

The very first target is written specification, not an emulator.
Correctness requires, in order: validated parse, unambiguous operand
types, proven permissible instruction selection, encoding evidence,
semantic tests including rejected illegal cases, and execution
on the selected target if claimed. The current revision has NOT
completed those stages.
